    <h1>Prop 19 Transfer Rules: How to Take Your Low Property Tax Base With You in California</h1>
    <div class="lede-line"></div>
    <div class="body">

      <p><strong>The short answer:</strong> if you're 55 or older, Proposition 19 lets you carry your existing Prop 13 property tax base to a replacement home anywhere in California, up to three times in your lifetime. You keep the low assessed value you've built over decades instead of being reassessed at today's market price. The rules are specific, so here's how it works in plain English.</p>

      <h2>Who qualifies for the Prop 19 base year value transfer?</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Age:</strong> you (or, if you're married, either spouse) must be at least 55 when you sell your original home. You can be under 55 when you buy the replacement.</li>
        <li><strong>Primary residence:</strong> the home you sell must be your principal residence, not a vacation home or rental, and the replacement must become your primary residence too.</li>
        <li><strong>Ownership:</strong> the property has to be owned by you personally. Homes held by a corporation or other legal entity don't qualify.</li>
        <li><strong>Other qualifying groups:</strong> Prop 19 also extends similar benefits to severely disabled homeowners and to victims of wildfire or other natural disasters.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How many times can you use it, and where?</h2>
      <p>Up to <strong>three times</strong> in your lifetime, even if you used an older Prop 60/90 transfer before Prop 19 took effect in 2021. The replacement home can be in <strong>any California county</strong>, so moving from Studio City to San Diego, Sacramento, or the coast doesn't cost you the benefit.</p>

      <h2>The timing rules</h2>
      <p>Two clocks matter, and people often mix them up:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Buying the replacement:</strong> it must be purchased or newly constructed within <strong>two years before or two years after</strong> you sell your original home.</li>
        <li><strong>Filing the claim:</strong> you file with the county assessor within <strong>three years</strong> of buying or completing the replacement. A late claim is still accepted, but the benefit only starts in the year you file, so you'd lose the savings in between.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Not every transaction fits neatly, so it's worth mapping your sale and purchase dates <em>before</em> you list.</p>

      <h2>How the numbers work</h2>
      <p>Your new tax base depends on the replacement home's market value compared with what your old home sold for. There's a small cushion that grows the longer you take to buy:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Bought before you sell:</strong> the cushion is 100% of your old home's market value.</li>
        <li><strong>Bought within a year after you sell:</strong> 105%.</li>
        <li><strong>Bought in the second year after you sell:</strong> 110%.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>If the replacement is at or below that threshold, your old tax base carries over (adjusted for the inflation factor). If it's above, only the <em>excess</em> is added to your base. You're taxed on the upgrade, not the whole new purchase.</p>

      <h3>A simple example (hypothetical numbers)</h3>
      <p>Say your longtime home sells for $1,500,000 and its current tax base is $300,000. You buy a replacement within a year after selling, so the threshold is 105% of $1,500,000, or $1,575,000.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>You buy at $1,400,000:</strong> under the threshold, so your base stays at about $300,000.</li>
        <li><strong>You buy at $1,800,000:</strong> $225,000 over the threshold, so your new base is about $525,000.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Compare that with a full reassessment at purchase price, and the annual difference can run to many thousands of dollars. This is a simplified illustration; the assessor applies inflation factors and exact dates, so your numbers will differ. California property tax is roughly 1% of assessed value plus local assessments.</p>

      <h2>How to file</h2>
      <p>You file <strong>Form BOE-19-B</strong>, <em>Claim for Transfer of Base Year Value to Replacement Primary Residence for Persons at Least Age 55 Years</em>, with the County Assessor in the county where your <em>replacement</em> home is located. The assessor's office can provide the form. Keep your closing documents for both homes handy.</p>

      <h2>Common mistakes to avoid</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Waiting to think about it until after you've sold.</strong> The two-year windows are generous, but planning early gives you the most options.</li>
        <li><strong>Assuming temporary reductions transfer.</strong> Prop 8 temporary value reductions and Mills Act values don't carry over to a replacement home.</li>
        <li><strong>Forgetting the claim.</strong> The benefit isn't automatic. You have to file.</li>
        <li><strong>Buying a replacement that's worth less than your current tax base.</strong> In that case, claiming the transfer doesn't help you.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What about an inherited home?</h2>
      <p>Prop 19 changed the rules for inherited property too. Heirs who want to keep a parent's low tax base generally have to use the home as their own primary residence; otherwise it's typically reassessed to current market value. If you're settling a family member's home, talk to your CPA or attorney early, and we'll work alongside them. Our <a href="/blog/selling-a-house-in-probate-california/">guide to selling a house in probate in California</a> covers the sale side.</p>

      <h2>Prop 19 questions we hear most</h2>
      <h3>Can I take my property taxes with me when I move in California?</h3>
      <p>Often, yes, if you're 55 or older (or severely disabled), you meet the primary residence rules, and you file the claim on time. You can use it up to three times.</p>
      <h3>Does the new home have to be in the same county?</h3>
      <p>No. The replacement home can be in any California county.</p>
      <h3>Does the new home have to cost less?</h3>
      <p>No. If it costs more, only the amount above the threshold is added to your tax base.</p>
      <h3>Do I need to buy before I sell?</h3>
      <p>No. You can buy up to two years before or two years after you sell. The threshold your new home is measured against rises slightly the longer you wait after selling, so the timing affects your numbers.</p>

      <p class="disclaimer">This page is general information based on the California State Board of Equalization's published guidance for transfers on or after April 1, 2021 (<a href="https://aws.boe.ca.gov/pdf/pub800-3.pdf" rel="noopener">Publication 800-3</a>), not tax or legal advice. Your county assessor and a qualified tax professional can confirm how the rules apply to you.</p>
